it's great to welcome to the program  today Sven Brinkman who is a professor  of psychology at the University of  Aalborg Denmark also author of books  including stand firm and the joy of  missing out spend it's really great to  speak to you today well I'm glad that  you invited me thank you  so let's talk a little bit about the joy  of missing out and sort of the other  side of that which is the fear of  missing out or FOMO as many Americans  now as sort of understand the term first  and foremost what are we really  describing when we talk about the fear  of missing out what's happening sort of  psychologically that influences people  yeah I mean the fear of missing out is  sort of a mentality that we have  developed in our consumer society I  would say with a constant doubt about  what we are doing what we are consuming  what we're buying who we are with in  marriages and relationships our jobs no  matter what we do we can post the  question to ourselves is this the right  thing for me and that you know gives  this fear the question am I missing out  on something that is more important that  that could potentially you know be  important to me realize my inner  potentials to to grow as a person or  something like that and in addition we  have developed as we all know now  technologies digital technologies in  particular social media that enable us  to access almost anything all the time  our relationships new use knowledge  about all sorts of things so all these  things taken together really gives us  this fear of missing out in the you know  short term but also in the longer term  in in our lives and it's deeply  dissatisfying to us it makes us unhappy  it makes us miserable and it's really  difficult to do something about because  it's  so ingrained in our culture and in our  technologies so I want to get in a  second to the way in which modern  technology is making this worse but  before we do that thinking back a little  bit is there any way that this fear of  missing out might have at some point in  human history been some kind of an  evolutionary advantage is that a  possibility yeah it certainly is it's  always a bit risky to speculate about  what life was like 200,000 years ago in  East Africa and you know we have all the  myths of evolutionary psychology  I mean I'm absolutely certain that we we  come from East Africa so to say but it's  not given how much we can actually take  from the knowledge of human beings as a  species and conclude about current life  today but I guess you're right that you  know our activities as explorers of this  world what's around the next hill is an  important evolutionary motor if you will  and of course this was developed in  times when resources were scarce where  we had to struggle and fight to to  obtain goods  and-and-and you know food and all sorts  of things that we need to survive but  now all these things are not scarce at  least not in most parts of the world we  have plenty of resources and I'm not  just talking about food but all sorts of  products knowledge is you know consumer  goods of all kinds so the problem is  really something else  it's how to develop the art of  self-restraint the art of missing out  and saying no to all these invitations  that we're constantly faced with yeah so  I mean like in my professional life at  several years ago I would basically say  yes to everything part of it was the  fear that whatever I turned down was  going to be like the  big break the opportunity to sort of get  to the next level and over time  partially because of the growth of my  program but also because I've just kind  of developed as a person I'm much more  comfortable saying no to things but what  I think might have been useful to me  seven or eight years ago would have been  some kind of a system to better that  evaluates when should you say yes and  when should you say no so like what what  might be an infrastructure that people  could apply to their lives to better  decide what it makes sense to do and  what it makes sense to pass on yeah no  this is an extremely important question  in my view we see now all sorts of  programs like you know simple living 10  or 15 years ago it's now being  rehabilitated we have Marie Kondo yes  talking about decluttering your life we  have books about you know the art of  saying no and so this has become quite a  trend and I think it's important I think  we should listen to these voices because  they really do speak to something  important in our times but what I would  like to see and what I hope will happen  is that we discuss these things with  some sort of ethical foundation with  some sort of you know value basis and  just to give one example and I I don't  mean to say anything you know about  Marie Kondo but the question she wants  us to pose when we pick up our objects  is does it spark joy in me and now this  is a good question in a way but what if  this joy is momentary or what if it  doesn't spark join me now but it might  tomorrow or I might have other reasons  to keep this object maybe because it  represents some symbolic value related  to my family or my history of whatever I  think we can only answer these questions  and develop the system that you rightly  longed for in a way if we understand  that humans have commitments we have  obligations and and these are  applications and commitments are  basically what structure or should  structure our lives if we are to develop  this art of self-restraint as I like to  call it it's it's not just a matter it's  not it's not just a matter of doing as  little as possible and saying no to all  sorts of things  it's about as I see it moderation  Palin balancing between too much on the  one side and too little on the other and  I really think that you know ethics  moral philosophy can help us arbitrate  and navigate in these matters one of the  terms that I've discussed before with my  audience is the the term hedonic  adaptation which is often applied to the  cost of one's lifestyle in other words  if you're used to a $50,000 a year  lifestyle and then you get a raise to  $100,000 and everything in your life is  adjusted to go with $100,000 lifestyle  at a certain point you will that will  become your new baseline and you will  not really experience any subjective  well-being that is different from the  $50,000 a year lifestyle does that term  apply to the fear of missing out and the  idea that if you start doing much more  that becomes your baseline and you don't  really feel any better yeah absolutely  and I do discuss this a bit in the book  the fact I should say that we strive for  something we we might obtain it and then  we get used to it and we just develop a  new baseline of happiness or contentment  or subjective well-being that seems to  be part of the human condition and now  we talked earlier about our evolutionary  past and in evolutionary terms this is  highly useful because it means that if  we experience some trauma some loss and  tragedy we actually most of us quite  quickly bounce back to the baseline  level and that's quite good for us so we  can keep on living and and in producing  and and you know the nurturing our  offspring and so on but it's not good  news when we talk about  peak experiences you know joys and and  good surprises like winning in the  lottery and stuff like that because  those joys are just as momentary and we  will quickly you know fall back to some  baseline of happiness and then we might  begin to wonder is something wrong with  me I just won the lottery I just got the  job that I wanted my girlfriend just  agreed to marry me and and I might be  happy for a week or two and then  everything is back to normal and I think  it will be very difficult to break free  from this hedonic treadmill as some  people also like to call it but I think  at least we can realize that it's a fact  of human nature it's a basic fact of our  psychology and then we might be content  you know we might not you know explode  with despair when realizing that why do  I not have these you know huge bursts of  happiness with all these good things  have happened to me well because that's  just life most of our of our lives we  live in some sort of middle gray area  concerning happiness and that's just as  it should be so maybe we shouldn't focus  as so much on you know super happiness  the subjective well-being maybe we  should focus more as I said before on  values on ethics on what is meaningful I  think that is a much more useful and  practical approach to life trying to  figure out what is meaningful how to  nurture your relationships how to live  up to the obligations we have to other  people and doing important projects in  our lives and then from time to time we  will feel these you know bursts of  happiness and joy but more as a side  effect I'm very suspicious that we can  you know chase these peak experiences of  happiness directly I think when they  appear they appear as side effects of  you know truly meaningful human  activities but this is mainly what my  book is about it has become very  difficult  to focus on these meaningful human  activities because of the fear of  missing out because of all the noise  that we hear you know all the  invitations we get from social media  from commercials in this consumer  society that we have created for  ourselves so in in the sort of limited  time that we have left can you now get  specifically into how social media and  the sort of scrolling social web sites  like Facebook and Twitter and Instagram  are worsening this problem yeah it's  sort of a technology designed like so  much else in our consumer societies to  to make us dissatisfied with what we  have we can never say when we scroll  down a you know Facebook feed or  something like that now I know enough  now I don't have to know anything else  because there's always something else to  see to you know to click to like to  explore and so it's really designed to  make us unhappy with what we haven't  what we see and to go and hunt more and  that is you know exploiting the general  mechanism that that we have as humans  and that we have seen in the past you  know 200 years really being articulated  and developed with consumer society that  if you're happy with what you have if  you're satisfied you really suffer from  you know advice it used to be a virtue  to be able to say well I'm happy I'm  content but if you're happy and content  then you no longer you know feed into  the machinery of the industry and so on  and so you need more you need something  else and yes the social media and the  digital technologies are simply just a  very visible symptom of that I remember  one ad from you know Intercontinental  Hotel the the you know big hotel chains  of hotel and I said you can't have a  favorite place until you've seen them  all and this is like the poetry of  capital  right it really tells us something true  in in quite a poetic form but also  something quite tragic because the  message really is you can never have a  favorite place because you can never see  them all and try to translate that into  jobs into life projects into  relationships you can never have a  favorite partner until you've had them  all well obviously that's meaningless  but maybe that's what we are thinking  because it's what we have been taught to  to think when developing this fear of  missing out we've been speaking with  Sven Brinkman who is professor of  psychology at the University of Aalborg  Denmark also author of the book stand  firm and the joy of missing out spend  really a pleasure having you on thank  you it was a pleasure to talk to you

